 1 big thing: Apple&#x27;s &quot;nothing broken&quot; quarter 
 
 By Nathan Bomey and Bradley Olson 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Apple CEO John Ternus. Photo: Adam Gray/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Apple delivered higher revenue than expected last quarter as the tech giant rides an uptick in iPhone sales but apparently disappointed investors with its outlook for the December period.

 By the numbers: The world's newly recrowned most valuable company posted revenue of $114.5 billion, up 11.7% compared to a year earlier.

 That topped S&amp;P Capital IQ consensus expectations of $113.4 billion. 📈 The intrigue: Apple's cautious approach to AI spending has been growing on investors, with its stock up about 25% in 2026.

 🤖 Catch up quick: Today's earnings report marks the first for John Ternus in his new role as CEO after he took the reins from Tim Cook, who is now executive chair.

 Investors are awaiting clues on how Ternus will guide Apple's AI strategy moving forward. 📱 💻 Zoom in: The company sold 57.58 million iPhones and 10.79 million Macs in the period, up 17.5% and 23.7%, respectively, from a year earlier.

 Sales in Apple's services business, which helped sustain its valuation in recent years under Cook's leadership, rose by almost 10%. In China, a key sales region for the company, Apple saw a sharp increase in revenue relative to somewhat tepid gains in recent years. Revenue from Greater China rose by more than 20% to $17.5 billion. 🤷 Yes, but: Apple fell about 4% in after-hours trading after the company guided growth in the December quarter between 7% and 10% year over year, citing supply constraints.

 🗣️ What they're saying: "There's nothing broken in this quarter, but there wasn't enough here to move the story forward either," writes Jake Behan, head of capital markets at Direxion. "Apple delivered the records traders expected but the stock's reaction says the recent move higher was already doing a lot of the work."

