 2. 🏃 Marvell gains Lumentum 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep ; Chart: Dan Primack/Axios Marvell Technology today announced that it will buy optical components maker Lumentum for $115 billion in stock.

 It's the largest merger ever between public tech companies — and a major vote of confidence in the AI data center boom, Axios' Dan Primack writes . 🥧 Between the lines: The value of this deal isn't primarily in cost savings (though the companies say they see a path to plenty of those), but in combining complementary technologies to capture a bigger slice of the AI spending pie, Goldman analysts wrote last week before the deal was confirmed, Axios' Pete Gannon writes.

 Marvell makes chips, while Lumentum specializes in the lasers and optical technologies that help move data between them using light. The bet is that bringing those capabilities under one roof will create a new kind of AI infrastructure supplier. "We are going to create a new category of company here," Marvell CEO Matt Murphy told CNBC . 📉 What we're watching: Investors will need some convincing — Marvell shares fell nearly 13% today. Lumentum slipped almost 19%.

 The bottom line: Investors are increasingly worried about the sustainability of the AI spending boom. Almost as worried as they are about missing out on it.

